[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Use Cases and Limitations of Reflection in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection in Java is a powerful feature that allows you to inspect and manipulate classes, interfaces, constructors, methods, and fields at runtime. It enables you to write code that can dynamically discover and use classes that might not even exist at compile time. However, reflection also has limitations and potential drawbacks that developers should be aware of."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Use Cases of Reflection"},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Dynamic Class Loading and Object Creation"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection makes it possible to load classes at runtime based on external configuration or user input. This is useful in scenarios where you don't know the specific classes you'll need until the application is running."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public class DynamicClassLoading {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Load a class by its name
            Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("com.example.MyClass");

            // Create an instance of the class
            Object obj = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

            System.out.println("Class loaded: " + clazz.getName());
            System.out.println("Object created: " + obj.getClass().getName());

        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("Class not found: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Error creating object: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

// Assuming you have a class like this:
package com.example;

public class MyClass {
    public MyClass() {
        System.out.println("MyClass constructor called");
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Examining and Modifying Class Members (Fields, Methods, Constructors)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection allows you to inspect the fields, methods, and constructors of a class, even if they are private. You can also modify the values of fields and invoke methods dynamically."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

public class ReflectionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("com.example.MyClass");
            Object obj = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

            // Accessing and modifying a private field
            Field privateField = clazz.getDeclaredField("privateVariable");
            privateField.setAccessible(true); // Disable access checks
            privateField.set(obj, "New Value");
            System.out.println("Private field value: " + privateField.get(obj));

            // Invoking a private method
            Method privateMethod = clazz.getDeclaredMethod("privateMethod", String.class);
            privateMethod.setAccessible(true);
            privateMethod.invoke(obj, "Hello");

        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}

// Updated MyClass:
package com.example;

public class MyClass {
    private String privateVariable = "Initial Value";

    public MyClass() {
        System.out.println("MyClass constructor called");
    }

    private void privateMethod(String message) {
        System.out.println("Private method called with: " + message);
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Framework Development (e.g., Dependency Injection, ORM)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Many Java frameworks, such as Spring and Hibernate, heavily rely on reflection. Dependency injection containers use reflection to instantiate and configure objects, while ORM frameworks use it to map Java objects to database tables."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Simplified example of a dependency injection container using reflection
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class DIContainer {

    public static void injectDependencies(Object obj) throws IllegalAccessException {
        Class<?> clazz = obj.getClass();
        Field[] fields = clazz.getDeclaredFields();

        for (Field field : fields) {
            if (field.isAnnotationPresent(Inject.class)) {
                field.setAccessible(true);
                // Assume we have a way to resolve the dependency based on the field type
                Object dependency = resolveDependency(field.getType());
                field.set(obj, dependency);
            }
        }
    }

    private static Object resolveDependency(Class<?> type) {
        // In a real DI container, this would involve more sophisticated logic
        // For simplicity, we just create a new instance of the required type
        try {
            return type.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to resolve dependency for type: " + type.getName(), e);
        }
    }
}

import java.lang.annotation.ElementType;
import java.lang.annotation.Retention;
import java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy;
import java.lang.annotation.Target;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.FIELD)
public @interface Inject {}

// Example usage:
class MyService {
    @Inject
    private MyRepository repository;

    public void doSomething() {
        repository.save("Data");
    }
}

class MyRepository {
    public void save(String data) {
        System.out.println("Saving data: " + data);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IllegalAccessException {
        MyService service = new MyService();
        DIContainer.injectDependencies(service);
        service.doSomething();
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Testing and Debugging"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection can be used to access and test private methods and fields, which is useful for unit testing and debugging. This allows you to verify the internal state of an object and ensure that it is behaving as expected."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import java.lang.reflect.Field;

class MyClass {
    private int internalState = 0;

    private void updateState(int value) {
        this.internalState = value;
    }

    public int getState() {
        return this.internalState;
    }
}

class MyClassTest {

    @Test
    void testPrivateMethodAndField() throws Exception {
        MyClass myClass = new MyClass();

        // Access private field
        Field field = MyClass.class.getDeclaredField("internalState");
        field.setAccessible(true);
        field.set(myClass, 10);

        // Verify the field value
        assertEquals(10, myClass.getState());

    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"Limitations of Reflection"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Performance Overhead"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection is significantly slower than direct code execution. The JVM needs to perform additional checks and lookups at runtime, which adds overhead.  Accessing fields and methods via reflection involves dynamic method resolution, which is inherently slower than static method calls. Avoid using reflection in performance-critical sections of your code."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class PerformanceTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        MyObject obj = new MyObject();
        long startTime, endTime;
        int iterations = 10000000;

        // Direct method call
        startTime = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            obj.directMethod();
        }
        endTime = System.nanoTime();
        System.out.println("Direct call time: " + (endTime - startTime) / 1000000 + " ms");

        // Reflection method call
        java.lang.reflect.Method method = MyObject.class.getMethod("directMethod");
        startTime = System.nanoTime();
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {
            method.invoke(obj);
        }
        endTime = System.nanoTime();
        System.out.println("Reflection call time: " + (endTime - startTime) / 1000000 + " ms");
    }
}

class MyObject {
    public void directMethod() {
        // Some simple operation
        int x = 1 + 1;
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Security Restrictions"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection can bypass normal access control mechanisms, potentially violating encapsulation. Security managers can restrict the use of reflection to prevent malicious code from accessing sensitive data or methods.  Granting reflection permissions should be done carefully and only when necessary."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"// Example of security manager preventing reflection
public class SecurityExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.setSecurityManager(new SecurityManager());

        try {
            Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("java.lang.System");
            java.lang.reflect.Method method = clazz.getDeclaredMethod("exit", int.class);
            method.invoke(null, 0); // Attempt to call System.exit
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Increased Complexity and Maintainability Issues"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Code that uses reflection can be harder to understand and maintain. The dynamic nature of reflection makes it difficult to reason about the code's behavior at compile time.  Errors related to reflection often occur at runtime, making them harder to debug.  Overuse of reflection can lead to brittle code that is difficult to refactor."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Potential for Illegal Access Exceptions"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"When working with modules (introduced in Java 9), reflection can be further restricted. If a module does not explicitly export a package, reflection may not be able to access types within that package, even if `setAccessible(true)` is used. This can lead to `IllegalAccessException` at runtime. You need to ensure modules are properly configured to allow reflection where necessary."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"// Example demonstrating IllegalAccessException with modules (requires Java 9+)
