"},{"order":28,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices and Conclusion"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reflection is a powerful tool, but it should be used judiciously. Consider the performance implications, security risks, and maintainability challenges before using reflection in your code. Whenever possible, prefer direct method calls and compile-time type checking. Use reflection only when necessary, such as in framework development or dynamic class loading scenarios. Always strive to minimize the scope of reflection and carefully handle potential exceptions."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use reflection sparingly, only when necessary."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be aware of the performance overhead."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider security implications and potential restrictions."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle exceptions carefully, especially `ClassNotFoundException` and `IllegalAccessException`."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Document your code clearly to explain why reflection is being used."}]
